  "summary": "A suspected China-based actor compromised three servers of a shared parks-and-recreation management platform by registering member accounts and abusing the member file upload feature to drop .aspx webshells, then used cmd.exe and PowerShell to enumerate IIS and hunt cardholder data. On return visits the actor renamed webshells as legitimate assets, timestomped them, and injected an obfuscated dropper into a jQuery file loaded by the authentication page to harvest credentials into WebRTC/WebSocket C2. Detection should focus on IIS worker processes spawning cmd.exe/PowerShell, webshell-named .aspx files, and the attacker's workers.dev C2 endpoints.",
